WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.197
Streaming: same work, sooner feedback.

2
00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:07.619
By default the SDK waits until the whole response is finished and then returns it.

3
00:00:07.899 --> 00:00:15.835
With streaming, the API sends server-sent events as tokens are produced, so your code can show text while the model is still writing.

4
00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:19.336
The total time to produce the response is about the same.

5
00:00:19.616 --> 00:00:26.805
What changes is time to first token, the delay before anything appears, which is what users perceive as responsiveness.

6
00:00:27.085 --> 00:00:36.131
For a long answer, the difference between a blank screen for twenty seconds and text appearing after one second is the difference between “broken” and “working.

7
00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:36.708
”.

8
00:00:36.988 --> 00:00:41.020
Streaming is also a reliability tool, not only a UX choice.

9
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:56.148
Anthropic’s docs recommend streaming (or the Batches API) for long requests, because some networks drop idle connections, and the Python SDK refuses non-streaming requests that it expects to exceed a ten-minute timeout.

10
00:00:56.428 --> 00:01:01.591
A useful default: stream whenever output might be long or max_tokens is large.

11
00:01:01.871 --> 00:01:11.535
The SDK’s stream helper accumulates the events for you and can hand back the same final message object a non-streaming call would, so you lose nothing.

12
00:01:11.815 --> 00:01:14.823
The raw event sequence is worth knowing for debugging.

13
00:01:15.103 --> 00:01:28.095
A stream starts with message_start (carrying the message shell and initial usage), then for each content block a content_block_start, a series of content_block_delta events with the new text, and a content_block_stop.

14
00:01:28.375 --> 00:01:35.842
A message_delta carries the stop_reason and final output token count (cumulative), and message_stop ends the stream.

15
00:01:36.122 --> 00:01:37.828
ping events may appear anywhere.

16
00:01:38.108 --> 00:01:48.199
And an error can arrive mid-stream, after the HTTP 200, as an error event such as overloaded_error, so stream handling needs its own error path.
